[{:seon.task/name "a-merge-writes-back-only-what-it-changed"
  :seon.task/plan "A form present in the source root but absent from the running binary is lost by a merge's write-back. Reproduction: bin/check 100's e2e-loop with target/seon built from main e117b9b and the source tree at a branch adding the private fn `seon.db/stored-id`: the loop's merge commit changed src/seon/fixtures.clj as asked and also deleted `stored-id` from src/seon/db.clj (12 lines), LOOP FAILED; on a binary rebuilt from the same source the loop passes (:files src/seon/fixtures.clj only). A binary older than its source is the normal case after a merge loads in place (docs/source.md#a-merge-loads-in-place), so this can delete merged code. Cause not found: the merge's delta, or the write-back's rendering of the file, treats the form as retracted. Correct: the write-back renders exactly the files whose forms the merge changed, from main's live forms, whatever the binary holds. The proof is not written."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.e2e-test/a-merge-on-an-older-binary-keeps-the-forms-it-lacks}}
 {:seon.task/name "test-namespaces-built-into-the-binary"
  :seon.task/plan "A root importing test/ restarted in 8.7-9.1 s against 3.5-3.6 s without it, and held 119-130 MB against 111 MB (`seon.lib/real-mb`; 382 namespaces against 333), measured with bin/system start at main 9382656 (before the changes below; not re-measured). Cause: the binary (`jolt build -m seon.main`) compiles src only, so a root with test/ in `:seon.config.source/dirs` (default [\"src\" \"test\" \"resources/seon/schemas\" \"resources/seon/config\" \"bin\"], resources/seon/config/default.edn, whose comment names this task for the +5 s a restart) evaluates the test forms one by one. What has moved since: the fork server holds the tests' load as due (`:tests-due` in `seon.fork-server`) and loads them at the first fork, not at the boot, and `bin/build` warms the binary's compile cache with the test namespaces (`seon.check/warm-tests`), so an agent does not compile them at its first fork. Still missing: the binary holds the test namespaces as it holds src's, so `seon.program/load-all!` finds them held and loads none. Check first what v1 did and whether Jolt's build takes a second source root (jolt build's :paths, a deps alias :test) without the test runner's main. Proof (not written): a restart of a root importing src and test/ costs no more than 1.2x a src-only restart, measured with `seon.profile/now-ns`. Why it matters (Step E's dry run, 2026-10-22): a worktree root on the default dirs cannot work any task whose proof belongs in an existing test namespace (the design agent could not load seon.e2e-test or seon.test-store)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-root-importing-test-restarts-as-fast-as-src}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-trees-commit-merges-three-way"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: when main's merged forms and a worktree session's edits to the same function meet at import, the tree wins: `seon.source/replace-tx` makes main's program the tree's, asked by an index, `seon.system/import-tree!` or `my.root/import!` (docs/source.md#indexing-a-tree). So a form an agent merged since the tree's commit is lost when the tree, unchanged there, is imported. Correct: the import is the tree's program merged into main by `seon.merge/three-way` against their merge base (main's program at the tree commit it last imported or wrote back), so only what the tree changed applies, and a both-sides change is an unmerged entry like any merge's. Deletes replace-tx's whole-program overwrite. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.source/seon-files-move-by-branch (DONE on main: edit! on any path; scripts and config as files of the program (`:file/*`, `[:script path]`, `[:config k]`, seon.fx/unreadable); a process reads config from its own branch; a boot names a file it can't load)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/an-import-keeps-a-form-main-merged-since-the-trees-commit}}
 {:seon.task/name "seon-reader-is-the-one-program-reader"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-03, on the bug-class audit read at c200a44aa9: \"make sure all of this fixes are applied and the docs are updated\"; by docs/merging.md#a-landing-message-carries-its-audit its report says what allowed the class, why the change makes it impossible, and which siblings it checked. What exists: program text is read by clojure.core/read under a bound *ns* in some places and by seon.reader in others, so ::alias/kw resolution depends on the call site (ece53cd25f moved one site). Still on clojure.core/read: seon.source/read-forms (used by accounts and by the defs map), ns-form, and three `read` calls in seon.repl (two functions; checked 2026-11-06 by grep); seon.config/parse-set and seon.reader swallow the read error and answer a generic message. The change: seon.reader is the one reader of program text; those sites use it; a read error is the reader's own, named; a bin/laws rule lists clojure.core/read and read-string in src as catch is listed. Docs: docs/source.md and docs/failure-points.md."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/an-unloaded-namespaces-alias-keyword-reads}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-schema-write-back-parses-the-whole-file"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-07: seon.source/schema-file-text costs 17.7 M ticks and 181 MB a call (377 calls), rewrite-clj parsing a whole schema resource to edit one entry, against an estimate of 0.5 M and 2 MB. Find the entry's form by its key and replace that span. Proof: a cost proof at a file's size, and every other byte of the file kept."
