 {:seon.task/name "a-task-changed-in-the-db-is-written-back-to-its-file"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-09: \"From that point on we are syncing data TO files not reading from them\"; root, 2026-11-14: tasks are edited through my.task in the store and the system writes their files; no session does file surgery (today: 15 store-only tasks, two dropped neighbour lines).\nWHAT EXISTS [M, read 2026-11-14]: the one write-back (seon.reconcile's :write-back, seon.source/files over seon.source/changed-files, committed by seon.fx/git-commit) writes each file kind seon.source/file-kinds names, :schema the model: its file entity holds its text, and entries-file-text rewrites only the entries main changed (docs/source.md#a-program-file). The one merge now merges a task's content as its [:task id] key (seon.merge's stated-keys). seon.source/task-file-text already edits a task file in place (rewrite-clj; my.root/sync! uses it). Task files are read by seon.task/load-tx, a second loader beside the import.\nTHE BUILD (the gate: the existing mechanism is the file kind; nothing new): task files become one more file kind, \"resources/seon/tasks/*.edn\" :task in :seon.config.source/things. :tx is the file with its text, as :schema's, plus load-tx's task facts; :text is task-file-text over the held text with an edit per task whose declaration on main differs from what the file states (:seon.task/declared), a removed task's entry removed. changed-files maps a [:task id] change to its owning namespace's file. So the merge that changes a task writes its file with the code it merged, and nothing else writes task files.\nESTIMATE: a merge touching k tasks in f files: f rewrite-clj passes over a file (largest ~120 KB, ~50 entries: ~5M ticks each) plus k declarations encoded; O(f), independent of the store.\nDELETES: my.root/sync!'s task-file path (the write-back does it), seon.task/load-tx reading files outside the import (the import's :tx), the :unsynced row's task half once no task can be store-only.\nPROOFS, red first: a task file indexed then drawn from main is a zero diff; a merge whose branch changed a task's plan writes that file and no other; a task created on a branch appears in its owning namespace's file after the merge."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-task-made-on-a-branch-appears-in-its-file-after-the-merge seon.source-test/a-merge-that-changed-a-task-writes-its-file-and-no-other seon.source-test/task-file-indexed-then-drawn-main-zero-diff} :seon.task/needs #{"seon.source/importing-a-task-file-keeps-the-branch-the-system-set" "seon.system/a-gates-candidate-carries-the-branchs-own-task-entries"}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-test-runners-kept-programs-are-keyed-by-their-indexing-code"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by the review of the build's kept index (2026-11-09): test-runner/program-db keeps one branch per git tree in the program store (:seon.config.proofs/program-store), brought to a tree with seon.source/refresh-tx from the nearest kept one, and keys those branches by `importer`, the digest of the whole text of src/seon/source.clj and src/seon/program.clj. That key is coarse (any edit of either file imports every tree again) and misses the code they call outside those files (the reader, seon.reader; Jolt's analyzer, by the runtime pins), so a kept branch can hold what other code made of a file: the class seon.system/kept-index! closes for the build's index and the process store (docs/source.md#the-binary-records-its-program). Correct: the branches are keyed by seon.program/index-code over the kept program (the reach of seon.program/index-roots, with the runtime pins), read and compared as kept-index! does, and `importer` is deleted. Proof, red first: a kept tree indexed under one reach digest, then the reach moved with the tree's files unchanged, plans a fresh import. Merged from seon.program/a-kept-tree-is-keyed-by-the-text-of-two-files (2026-11-10 triage): same cause (the kept tree keyed by the text of source.clj and program.clj); that task gains the measurements and the done rule (a comment edit and a digest-only edit to seon.program each plan in under 300 M ticks and match a whole import datom by datom), and importer and builder-id become one key"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/kept-tree-indexed-under-reach-digest-then-reach}}
 {:seon.task/name "privacy-comes-from-the-accounts"
  :seon.task/plan "Owed by jolt/index-arg (landed aa08b56b21, which completed seon.source analysis-never-writes-the-image): seon.source/tree-private answers a var's privacy for the analysis env's :private? by reading each changed namespace's forms a second time (text-privacy, recognizing defn- and ^:private by the form's head). read-nss already analyzes the files in load order and each account carries the definition's meta, so the privacy of a namespace analyzed earlier can come from its accounts, and text-privacy goes. The edge to state: a file naming a var of a later file in load order without requiring it. Estimate: index-arg was estimated at +10 to +15 src lines and measured +79 (ix threaded through readers' signatures); this one should delete about 10. Also owed from it, Web's in 2a: the :own-index? flag goes when every walk is handed its index."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/analysis-env-s-private-var-earlier-analyzed-namespace}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-macro-bakes-the-analyzed-trees-files"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-11 by the Tests session (jolt/tests-baked, landed in batch 57). A macro that reads a file when it expands (seon.config's baked-schema-forms and baked-shipped-text; the reads ```seon.source/file-reading-expansions``` finds) reads it from the RUNNING checkout, so analysing another tree's program (health's base, a kept tree refreshed from another) bakes the running checkout's files, not that tree's. Health's base is now built by the base's own code in its own process (jolt/tests-healthbase), which closes it for health only. Also: deps.edn and resources/seon/system.edn are not program files (test-runner/program-file?), so a change to either never refreshes the text ```seon.fork-server``` and ```seon.graph``` bake from them. Correct: a file-reading macro reads the analyzed tree's file (the tree's text the db holds, as refresh-tx's file-text does), and every file such a macro reads is a refresh input. Proof: analysing a tree whose schema differs from the running checkout's bakes the tree's schemas; a system.edn-only change refreshes the baking form. Absorbed in curation 2026-11-14 (same subject and evidence): seon.source/another-trees-ns-form-analyzes-beside-the-image (A second case of the class, 2026-11-05: a branch moved seon.turn/call-problem to seon.repl, and main's test naming it with var made the base tree's analysis throw in the branch's image, failing bin/check health with no record of why; the test was changed to resolve it when run, which works around it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-macro-bakes-the-analyzed-trees-files}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-moves-gits-main-to-the-stores-files"
  :seon.task/plan "Plan Stage 1.7, git follows the store. Today write-back commits to jolt/merges (157 commits ahead of main, same tree) and my.root/sync! expects that branch to be landed with bin/land, so the loop runs back through the old path. After Seon's merge, one :git effect commits main's changed files to git's main as a compare-and-swap of the ref, once root has read the diff; a moved ref is a typed error, never an overwrite. Deletes jolt/merges as an indirection, sync!'s bin/land step and bin/land's bridge, after an audit to the owner. Owners: runtime, root."
