 {:seon.task/name "a-schema-key-defined-in-two-files-is-refused-at-import"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data (the import of schema files; runtime found it). Seen 2026-11-15 17:4x [M runtime's worktree health, its store moved aside to /tmp/rt4/program-store-health-base-13b11955.stale]. jolt/runtime 3e0dd0ec2d defined :seon.system/update in seon.facts.edn while seon.core.edn:280 already defined it, and nothing refused the second definition. :schema/key is unique, so the facts import took over the one entity. The next commit (0827872b07) removed the facts entry, and its import retracted the key, while core's definition stayed in its unchanged file, never re-read. From then on health said :unschemed-key :seon.system/update is named by 4 vars and no schema entry defines it, while root's main (imported fresh) holds it. The bug's class: an entity whose identity (:schema/key) two files claim, with the import diffing per file. Fix at the import: a key a second file defines is refused as a typed error naming both files, as the defs law refuses a name defined twice, so the second definition never lands; no retraction then ever drops a key another file holds. PRIOR WORK, to search first: my.schema/register! and registration (my/schema.clj:60-110, :schema/file at :84), seon.source's catch-up of changed files, and the defs law (bin/laws, every top-level name defined once). COST: one :schema/key seek per entry imported, which the import already does."
