 {:seon.task/name "every-mirrored-branch-is-written-back-to-its-git-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data, with context for the import side. The owner (root, 2026-11-16): work that passes and merges in Seon becomes a git commit on git's main, and every Seon branch with a session worktree is mirrored onto that worktree's jolt/<area>, one history but for a local edit's lag; one write-back for every branch, no second mechanism, and no review gate. PRIOR WORK, read: the one write-back, the reconciler's :writeback flight (reconcile.clj ~1320-1355) asking the :git effect (seon.fx/git-commit, fx.clj:822) to commit main's changed files (seon.source/changed-files, file-text, schema-resource-text) by plumbing on :seon.config.source/branch (#seon/own-branch \"jolt/seon-merges\", default.edn:585), moving the ref only from the expected commit, through the worktree when the branch is checked out (refused when that tree holds its own edits), and moving main's :seon.system/source in the same write (seon.reconcile/source-moved-tx); the gate after it, my.root/sync! and sync-req (root.clj:260-288, a jolt/sync-<name> branch reviewed and landed by bin/land), and the tree's catch-up, my.root/merges-caught-up! (root.clj:210) with bin/update's merge of main into jolt/merges. DESIGN: (1) a mirrored branch's source fact names its git branch, {:git-ref :commit :tree :t}: main's :seon.system/source generalized to one per mirrored Seon branch (git's main for main, jolt/<area> for task-session-<area>); (2) each commit that moves a mirrored branch's program (the writer already judges it, seon.program/moves-program?) asks the write-back for that branch: its changed files since the source fact's :t, committed on the fact's ref with :expect its :commit, through the worktree that has it checked out, the fact moved in the same write; commits arriving meanwhile are one write-back of the latest head; (3) git's main is written directly, so a merged task's files are on main when it merges, :unsynced is only the write-back's lag, and root reviews after (decisions: root reviews after the merge); (4) the import side, agreed with context: a worktree edit is imported first (bin/seon-import, my.branch/import!) as a Seon commit, whose write-back commits the same bytes, so the tree is clean; a worktree holding edits not yet imported refuses, typed, and the next import's commit writes it. DELETES: jolt/merges and .worktrees/merges, the #seon/own-branch jolt/seon-merges-<worktree> refs, my.root/sync! and sync-req and the jolt/sync-* branches, my.root/merges-caught-up! and merge-moved-tx, bin/update's step 1 (main into the write-back tree), the review gate; :seon.config.source/branch becomes each fact's :git-ref. Kept: seon.source's byte-for-byte file text, the CAS by :expect, the checked-out-tree path. ESTIMATE per write-back, f the changed files: file text O(forms of each changed file), ~1-5 ms a file [H]; git plumbing, a hash-object per file plus mktree, commit-tree and update-ref, ~5-10 ms a git process [H, to measure], ~30 ms for a few files; records-only commits cost nothing (no program move); lower bound O(f). DURING THE MOVE: bin/land also moves git's main; the two writers are ordered by the ref's compare-and-swap, and a write-back of bytes main already holds commits nothing (import then write-back is a zero diff, seon.source-test/import-then-write-back-is-a-zero-diff). AGREED with context (2026-11-16): the import lands on the session's own agent (session/<area>, found from the worktree's jolt/<area>, never a checkout-<path> client: context's fix first); a write-back into a worktree that already holds the bytes rewrites no file but still commits them on jolt/<area> (index and HEAD, CAS from the fact's :commit) and moves the fact, so the git branch mirrors every Seon commit; a no-op only when HEAD already holds them: HEAD's tree is compared with the bytes first, and when equal (a session's hand commit of the same bytes moved HEAD) nothing is committed and the fact moves to HEAD, never a refusal; a HEAD holding other bytes is a commit Seon hasn't imported, which the import takes (context's). The import's shape (context, landing): my.branch/import! is one write through the agent's own writer, an eval at the session agent's REPL that is no turn, the changed files as program facts as an eval writes them (seon.system/catch-up :fact? false :paths); it moves no source fact, so the write-back's fact alone names the git commit. ROOT (2026-11-16, tests' measurement): after a boot or a land, the writer's :program-t (writer.clj:515) runs ahead of main's source fact's :t (my.root/landed-tx, system.clj:1455), so main reads behind until the next write-back. By construction here: a mirrored branch's source fact moves in the same commit as the write-back that writes its files, and a program move on main always asks a write-back, so the fact's :t lags :program-t only by the write-back in flight, never by an unasked one; a land's import writes the fact's :t as the t it imported (the files equal main's then). Proof, red first: seon.source-test/a-merge-to-main-is-a-commit-on-gits-main."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-merge-to-main-is-a-commit-on-gits-main}
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.fx{git-commit {:n 5, :order :linear, :ticks 20000000, :bytes 20000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-script-edit-costs-one-entity"
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data (the import). Found by context, 2026-11-16 [M at root]: seon.system/changed-tx of one changed script (bin/land, a line added) costs 43.8M ticks and 143 MB [result/b5d63c29]; of that, seon.source/refresh-tx of {\"bin/land\" text} is 37.1M ticks and 104 MB [result/c6a10d38], while seon.source/edit-tx of the same text, the existing writer of an allow-list kind, is 65k ticks and 0.26 MB [result/d0260a0f]: 570x for one entity. The class: a file of a kind seon.source/file-kinds names reaches refresh-tx, which is for namespaces. PRIOR WORK: file-kinds (source.clj:300), edit-tx (source.clj:539), changed-tx's program? pick (system.clj:1389). Cause first: read what refresh-tx walks for a path that holds no namespace (both ends), then route every kind through its :tx in changed-tx and let refresh-tx see namespaces only. Proof: seon.source-test/a-script-edit-costs-one-entity, a cost proof of changed-tx on one script at two program sizes, flat. COST: bound one entity's facts, ~0.1M ticks."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/a-script-edit-costs-one-entity}
  :seon.task/estimate {seon.system/changed-tx {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 200000, :bytes 2000000}}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-imported-bare-key-is-declared"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: data. A schema file's bare key (an attribute whose schema is its stored value, no entity map listing it) imported onto a branch or main is never declared in Datahike: seon.system/changed-tx declares only what seon.program/schema-declarations finds (sd/declarations of the registry: entity maps' attributes), so the branch cannot store the key, though a registration of it can (seon.program/own-declaration). Measured: changed-tx of {:app.shape/size :int} on an empty world leaves (d/schema db) without :app.shape/size (result/be78e1ab). The deleted merge-tx declared it from the process registry at merge time; the one merge carries only the branch's own declarations (seon.writer/replay), so the gap now shows at the import, where it belongs.\nPRIOR WORK: seon.program/own-declaration (a registration's), seon.program/schema-declarations, seon.system/changed-tx's decls, my.branch/import!.\nPLAN: changed-tx (and the agent's import path) declare each bare key the changed schema files add with own-declaration, beside schema-declarations, so an import writes what a registration writes.\nESTIMATE: O(keys the changed files add), one registry lookup each, ~10k ticks a key.\nPROOF, red first: an import of a schema file adding a bare key leaves the key storable on the branch."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.source-test/an-imported-bare-key-is-declared}
  :seon.task/needs #{}
  :seon.task/estimate {seon.system/changed-tx {:n 1, :order :linear, :ticks 10000, :bytes 100000}}}]
